[Music]  this is your tech news briefing for  monday july 18th i'm zoe thomas for the  wall street journal  by now it's common knowledge that  someone somewhere is after your data  advertisers want information on your  interest so they can sell you things  law enforcement might be interested in  your search history texts or location  data  hackers well they want your data to  steal things  and following the supreme court's  decision to overturn roe v wade more  people are thinking about how that data  could be used against them if they  sought an abortion we covered that a  couple weeks ago on the show so if  you're interested be sure to queue that  episode up next  but the issue of who has access to your  data and how they could use it raises  another question  who are you choosing to share it with  the answer may surprise you and it  depends a lot on what apps you have on  your phone  joining us to discuss that and how to  protect your personal data on your phone  is our personal tech columnist nicole  nguyen  hey nicole thanks for joining us hi  thanks for having me nicole let's start  with all the sensors that are used by  apps are apps limiting themselves to the  information that they need to run or are  they collecting information that  maybe might seem a little excessive if  we were to look at it it really depends  on the app we hope that apps only ask  for access and actually do access stuff  on our phone that they need to run  but some  don't and that's because they're  maybe they're free and they earn their  revenue by sending data about people who  use their app to marketers so for  example if you download a free game or  something seemingly innocuous like a  flashlight app and it's using a lot of  stuff on your phone you know you notice  that in the battery usage data analysis  that you can get in settings on your  phone that the flashlight app is one of  the top ones then  maybe double check and look into why  something  that isn't running all the time 